<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galápagos Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Galápagos Islands are a volcanic archipelago in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> about 1,000 km west of the coast of Ecuador, which annexed them in 1832. Their unique wildlife, including giant tortoises, marine iguanas and finches, influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>'s thinking on evolution after his visit in 1835. They became Ecuador's first national park in 1959 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands.jpg" alt="Galápagos Islands" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lobo_marino_(Zalophus_californianus_wollebaeki),_Punta_Pitt,_isla_de_San_Crist%C3%B3bal,_islas_Gal%C3%A1pagos,_Ecuador,_2015-07-24,_DD_11.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
